[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/build/` directory, which the build instructions call for.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 1. Blank lines re-executed the previous command
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Any input containing a blank or whitespace-only line diverged.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Given `whoami\n\nstatus\n\n`, the C printed `Not logged in` once; the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printed it twice and printed the status block twice.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `process_command` declares `char command[MAX_COMMAND]` and only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writes it when `parse_command` finds a token:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m char *token = strtok(temp, " \t");
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if (token) { strncpy(cmd, token, MAX_COMMAND - 1); ... }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so a line with no tokens leaves `command` holding whatever was last in that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stack slot, and `strlen(command) == 0` decides whether the line is a no-op. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust modelled this as a *persistent* buffer, i.e. it assumed the stale value was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the previous iteration's command name.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m That assumption is wrong for the reference build. Between two calls to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `process_command`, `main` calls `printf` and `fgets`, whose frames are far
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deeper than `process_command`'s and overwrite that region. In the reference
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m binary the slot always reads back with a NUL at byte 0, so `strlen` is 0 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **every** token-less line is a no-op, regardless of what preceded it. Verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against the C across many preceding commands, including long ones (`help`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 64-byte command names, `compareN` with 24-byte operands).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `parse_command` clears `command` on entry, so a token-less line always
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m yields an empty command and returns immediately.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. The first `.bss` map was taken from the wrong build
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Overflow tests that passed against my initial reference binary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failed after rebuilding, and vice versa.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** My first reference build used `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`. At `-O3`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the linker emits the globals in a different order than at `-O0`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | build | order in `.bss` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O3` (wrong) | `variables`, `file_count`, `files`, `current_user`, `user_count`, `users` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O0` (**graded**) | `users`, `user_count`, `current_user`, `files`, `file_count`, `variables`, `variable_count`, `debug_mode`, `verbose_mode` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Since the observable effect of an overflow past the end of an array is entirely
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m determined by *which global sits next*, the two builds behave differently on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same input. The graded build is the unoptimised one.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Rebuilt without a build type and re-derived the map from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `nm build/driver`. The emulated layout in `src/main.rs` (see the `BSS MAP`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comment) now uses the `-O0` addresses:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x4070a0  users           720 bytes (10 * 72)   -> ends 0x407370
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x407370  user_count      int
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x407378  current_user    user_t *
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x407380  files         12240 bytes (20 * 612)  -> ends 0x40a350
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x40a350  file_count      int
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x40a360  variables      3200 bytes (20 * 160)  -> ends 0x40afe0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x40afe0  variable_count / debug_mode / verbose_mode
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0x40aff0  _end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Struct sizes and field offsets were confirmed against a `sizeof`/`offsetof`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m probe compiled with the same toolchain: `user_t` 72 (0/32/64/68), `file_t` 612
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (0/64/576/608), `variable_t` 160 (0/32).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 3. Overflow past an array end did not fault
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Two input classes made the C die from `SIGSEGV` (shell status 139)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with **no output at all**, while the Rust ran to completion and exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The C copies up-to-63-byte tokens into 32-byte fields with `strcpy`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m For the last element of an array, that overflow lands on the global that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m immediately follows — and per the map above, that global is an array *counter*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which the very next statement uses as a subscript:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m strcpy(users[user_count].password, args[1]);          /* overruns user_count  */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m users[user_count].permission_level = ...;             /* wild store           */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m strcpy(files[file_count].owner, current_user->name);  /* overruns file_count  */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m files[file_count].permissions = 755;                  /* wild store           */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because the reference is unoptimised, every mention of `user_count` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `file_count` is a fresh load, so the clobbered value is the one used to index.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed with `stdbuf -o0`: the C dies inside `adduser` / `createfile` itself,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not at a later read.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two concrete triggers:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - a 63-byte password on the tenth user sets `user_count` to `0x50505050`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`"PPPP"`), then stores to `users[1347440720].permission_level`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - an over-long file owner on the twentieth file writes `"et\0"` over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `file_count` (making it 29797), then stores to `files[29797].permissions`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m An owner becomes over-long through finding 1's sibling bug: a 40-byte username
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fills `name[32]` and runs into `password[32]`, so `current_user->name` reads
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m back as 32 bytes of username followed by the password — 38 bytes for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `adduser AAAA…(40) secret`, which is longer than the 32-byte `owner` field.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust instead modelled each array as its own `Vec` with a zero-filled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SLACK` tail, so these writes were absorbed harmlessly and no counter was ever
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m corrupted.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Replaced the three separate buffers with a single flat emulation of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whole `.bss` page range (`0x407000`–`0x40b000`) at the real symbol addresses.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The counters, `current_user`, `debug_mode` and `verbose_mode` now live *in* that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffer and are re-read from it at every use, so an overflow corrupts them
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exactly as in C. Any access outside the mapped range raises a real `SIGSEGV`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with the default disposition, so the process is killed by signal 11 and writes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m nothing to stderr, matching the C.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note this also reproduces the *non*-crashing corruption: a 40-byte password on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the tenth user puts its NUL on `user_count`'s low byte, zeroing the count so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m subsequent writes and the increment operate on index 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 4. stdout buffer size changed what survived a crash
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** On a crashing input with several KB of preceding output, the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m emitted 4096 bytes and the Rust emitted a different amount.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** When the process dies from a signal, whatever is still in the stdio
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffer is lost, so the buffer size decides how much an observer sees. glibc
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m gives a pipe a **4096-byte** fully-buffered stream and writes it out in whole
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m blocks; the Rust used a 64 KiB `BufWriter`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Replaced it with an explicit 4096-byte buffer flushed in whole blocks.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 5. `std::io::Stdout` held back the trailing partial line
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** After fix 4, on the same input the C emitted exactly 4096 bytes but
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the Rust emitted 4060. The first 4060 bytes were identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** My 4096-byte block was being written *into* `std::io::Stdout`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m interposes its own `LineWriter`. That buffer flushes on newlines, so it passed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through only the complete lines of the block and retained the trailing 36-byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m partial line — which was then lost to the signal.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `raw_write` hands each block straight to `write(2)` on fd 1, looping on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m partial writes. `std::io::Stdout` is not used at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 6. Debug builds could panic where `-O0` C wraps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Latent, not observed in a passing input. `cargo test` builds the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m debug profile, in which Rust panics on integer overflow.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Once a counter can be clobbered to an arbitrary `i32` (finding 3),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m expressions like `file_count - 1` and `user_count + 1` can overflow. A panic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m would print to stderr and exit 101, matching neither the C's output nor its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status. Input bytes are unrestricted, so a token containing `0x80`/`0xff` can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plant such a value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Counter and loop-induction arithmetic uses `wrapping_add` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `wrapping_sub`, which is what gcc emits at `-O0`. (`arg_count` and `match`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m counters are bounded by 10 and left as-is.)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours checked and already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed by test and by fuzzing, so recorded here to document that they were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *verified* rather than assumed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `strcmp`/`strncmp` return the difference of the first differing bytes taken as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `unsigned char`, printed with `%d` (e.g. `compare ~ A` → 61, `compare a \xff`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → negative).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `atoi` is `(int) strtol`, so `99999999999999999999` saturates to `LONG_MAX`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and truncates to `-1`, and `2147483648` truncates to `-2147483648`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `compareN` passes a possibly-negative `int` as `strncmp`'s `size_t`: the value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   sign-extends into a huge length but still prints as negative.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `fgets` caps a line at 255 bytes and leaves the remainder to be read as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   next line, so one long line becomes several commands.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `strcspn(input, "\n")` strips only the first newline, so a `\r` from CRLF
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   input survives into the token.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `strtok` on `" \t"` collapses delimiter runs and drops empty fields;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `MAX_ARGS` caps arguments at 10; `strncpy` truncates each token to 63 bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Within-record overflow: a 40-byte username makes `listusers` report
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `AAAA…(32)` + the password; a 48-byte password is truncated by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    210[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `permission_level` assignment that follows it, so login with the original
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    211[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   password fails.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    212[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Cross-record overflow: an over-long owner overruns `permissions` and the next
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    213[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   record's filename, so `listfiles` prints the raw little-endian bytes of 755
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    214[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`\xf3\x02`) as part of the owner. Output is byte-oriented throughout, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    215[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   non-UTF-8 bytes pass through unchanged.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    216[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Command dispatch order, including that the `strncmp` "Did you mean" branches
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    217[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   are only reached after every `strcmp` fails, and all aliases (`users`,
