3. [1mOut-of-array overflow didn't fault.[22m The Rust gave each array a zero-filled slack tail, absorbing writes harmlessly. In C, [38;5;10musers[0m is immediately followed by [38;5;10muser_count[0m and [38;5;10mfiles[0m by [38;5;10mfile_count[0m, so an overflowing [38;5;10mstrcpy[0m clobbers the very counter the next statement uses as a subscript — a wild store that kills the process. Two triggers: a 63-byte password on the 10th user, and an over-long file owner on the 20th file. Verified with [38;5;10mstdbuf -o0[0m that C dies inside [38;5;10madduser[0m/[38;5;10mcreatefile[0m itself.[0m[0m
